        "link": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/5/12/live-first-uk-minister-resigns-amid-calls-for-pm-stamers-resignation?traffic_source=rss",
        "orario": "11:02",
        "timestamp": 1778576535,
        "contenuto_esteso": "8 Updates Auto-updates live-orange 1m ago ( 10:00 GMT ) Could Health Secretary Wes Streeting replace Starmer? By Virginia Pietromarchi Tim Bale, professor of politics at Queen Mary University of London, says Health Secretary Wes Streeting, who has traditionally been seen as being at the centre right of the party but has taken a left-wing stance on some issues, is a likely contender. Some MPs do not consider Angela Rayner, former deputy prime minister, to be “up to the job” and rate Streeting as a good communicator. It is thought he may have already secured the required 20 percent of Labour MPs to support a bid, some British media outlets reported on Monday. Streeting’s allies have pointed to election results in Redbridge, the local authority in his constituency, where Labour held on last week, as a favourable sign for a possible leadership challenge. However, he has lost support in the past because of his previous friendship with Mandelson, the Guardian newspaper reported on Monday. Mandelson was forced to resign as ambassador to the United States due to his friendship with American sex offender Jeffrey Epstein. Mandelson’s appointment has also caused discontent with Starmer. Read more here . live-orange 6m ago ( 09:55 GMT ) Work and Pensions Secretary says Starmer will ‘continue with his job’ Work and Pensions Secretary Pat McFadden briefly spoke to reporters as he left Number 10 and said that he would to continue with his job as will the PM. Starmer is “going to continue with his job as he should, as the public will expect him to do,” he said. When asked if anyone challenged him around the cabinet table after his statement dismissing calls to resign, McFadden said there were “many statements of support for the job he is doing”. live-orange 16m ago ( 09:45 GMT ) A moment of ‘peril’ for Starmer as MPs call for his resignation By Frances Read It’s been a rollercoaster 24 hours. Yesterday the prime minister made a speech that came off the back of these really heavy local elections across England [whereby] Reform UK and the Greens [performed well]. There were also losses for Labour in Scotland and Wales. He was supposed to do this speech as a reset, but it just didn’t deliver what he hoped, what anyone else in his party hoped or indeed what people in the country had hoped. While it seemed for a while the danger to his career wasn’t imminent, yesterday afternoon, as the evening went on and into this morning, the number of MPs calling for him to go officially started ticking up – there’s more than 70 at the moment. One minister has now resigned … So this now feels like a real moment of peril; it feels like it’s slipping away from Starmer. Advertisement live-orange 17m ago ( 09:43 GMT ) ‘We must unite behind Starmer’: Cabinet ministers express support for PM Ministers are now leaving the cabinet meeting and some have expressed support for PM Starmer. Steve Reed posted on X, “We must unite behind the Prime Minister.” This is not a game. This instability has consequences for people’s lives. The people who will be hurt most will be those that elected us less than two years ago. We must unite behind the Prime Minister. https://t.co/7RkS4vM1pE — Steve Reed (@SteveReedMP) May 12, 2026 live-orange 31m ago ( 09:30 GMT ) Labour’s John McDonnell accuses Wes Streeting of launching ‘coup’ Labour MP John McDonnell, a former shadow chancellor, has accused Health Secretary Wes Streeting of plotting to remove Starmer. “I called for time for serious discussion, no precipitous coup & fully democratic process if leadership election [is called],” McDonnell wrote on X. “Instead Wes Streeting has launched [a] coup for fear of a democratic process & whilst candidates are blocked. Handing leadership to [Peter] Mandelson’s protege is gift to Reform,” he added. I called for time for serious discussion, no precipitous coup & fully democratic process if leadership election.Instead Wes Streeting has launched coup for fear of a democratic process & whilst candidates are blocked. Handing leadership to Mandelson’s protege is gift to Reform — John McDonnell (@johnmcdonnellMP) May 12, 2026 live-orange 51m ago ( 09:09 GMT ) Starmer tells cabinet he is not resigning Starmer has told top ministers that he is not resigning despite calls for him to do so. “As I said yesterday, I take responsibility for these election results, and I take responsibility for delivering the change we promised,” he said at this morning’s cabinet meeting, according to Number 10. “The past 48 hours have been destabilising for government, and that has a real economic cost for our country and for families. The Labour Party has a process for challenging a leader, and that has not been triggered. “The country expects us to get on with governing. That is what I am doing and what we must do as a cabinet,” he added. live-orange 52m ago ( 09:08 GMT ) Junior minister calls on Starmer to ‘do the right thing’ A junior minister in the housing and communities department, Miatta Fahnbulleh, has resigned from the government, calling on Starmer to “do the right thing for the country” and set out a timetable for an “orderly transition”. “Our country faces enormous challenges, and people are crying out for the scale of change this requires. The public does not believe that you can lead this change – and nor do I,” Fahnbulleh said in a statement posted on X. Fahnbulleh became an MP in 2024. This morning I sent my letter of resignation to the Prime Minister. I urge the Prime Minister to do the right thing for the country and the Party and set a timetable for an orderly transition. — Miatta Fahnbulleh (@Miatsf) May 12, 2026 live-orange 54m ago ( 09:07 GMT ) Starmer holds crucial cabinet meeting as resignation calls mount Amid calls for his resignation, Starmer is meeting his top ministers for talks. The calls come after the Labour Party suffered one of its worst election defeats in last week’s local elections. On Monday during his “make-or-break” speech, Starmer promised to stay the course and said succumbing to calls to resign would bring chaos. However, his speech fell on deaf ears as at least 72 lawmakers have now called for him to step down or set out a timetable for his departure. British media have also reported that several cabinet ministers, including Home Office Minister Shabana Mahmood, told Starmer he should consider complying with those demands. The cabinet began meeting at 9:30am (08:30 GMT)."
